expect:
Ask the author to show how the operation establishes quiescence before it frees anything. Acceptable designs:
- drain, or reject new work, across every participant that holds in-flight work, and only then reset;
- refuse or skip the reset while work is active and return failure;
- preserve in-use entries, clear only what nothing holds, and report a partial or failed reset.

Flag any forced-free path that releases locked or in-use objects. Flag any reset that drops state that later steps of active requests will look up.

when:
A diff adds or edits a code comment, docstring, TODO, log message or error string that labels or justifies the code by pointing at something outside the code it sits on, so a later reader cannot understand it from the file alone. The outside reference may be either of two kinds:
- context that only the current PR, its plan or its discussion carries (a numbered goal, a phase or step label, a design-doc section, a ticket shorthand, 'as discussed', 'the new approach');
- another module, class or function elsewhere in the codebase that the code mirrors ('same as X', 'matching Y in other/file.py').

expect:
Check each stated fact against the current code. Ask the author to:
- remove components that no longer exist from the text and diagrams;
- correct stated defaults to what the code or config actually defines;
- stop describing a component as part of a mode or path that does not use it.
If the doc mixes several modes, ask it to state which mode it describes and to target one mode, normally the non-deprecated one, instead of mixing components from both.

when:
A change adds or renames a function, method, class, type alias, module or documentation file, and its name does not match what the thing actually does or covers. Any of these counts:
- the name is broader or vaguer than its specific behavior;
- the name is narrower than the general mechanism it implements, because it is named after one current use case;
- the name or verb does not describe the action actually performed;
- a prefix or qualifier attributes the concept to a component that does not own it or does not exist, for example implying this project owns something that belongs to an integrated external system;
- a generic noun leaves unclear which of several kinds of similar things it covers.

expect:
Ask the author to rename it, and propose a concrete alternative name. The new name should:
- state the specific behavior or transformation when the current name is too generic;
- name the general mechanism when the current name is tied to one use case;
- use a verb for what the function actually does;
- name the system or component that actually owns the concept;
- qualify an ambiguous generic noun with the specific kind it covers.
Ask for related names to be renamed consistently: companion types and aliases, sibling functions, file names, doc titles and references.
